def _src(key, short, t, by, url, note_en, note_cy):
    return key, dict(short=short, t=t, by=by, note=(note_en, note_cy), url=url, lang="en")


LONG.update({
 # ---------- Roman Wales: fuller pages ----------
 "caerleon": (
  ["## Isca, the fortress of the Second Legion",
   "Caerllion on the Usk was Isca, one of only three permanent legionary fortresses in Roman Britain.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerleon]] It was the home of the Second Augustan Legion for some two hundred years. From here the legion held the Silures, who had fought Rome longer than almost any people in Britain.",
   "Here you can still see the fortress baths, whose open-air swimming pool once held more than 80,000 gallons of water; the amphitheatre, the most complete in Britain, with room for about 6,000; and the soldiers’ barracks, the only legionary barracks still on view anywhere in Europe.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerleon]]",
   "## Arthur’s city",
   "Caerleon was never forgotten. Gerald of Wales saw its great walls, baths and palaces still standing in 1188, and Welsh tradition holds that Arthur held court here: the three Welsh romances of Owain, Peredur and Gereint all begin with Arthur at Caerllion ar Wysg. Gildas tells of Julius and Aaron, martyred in the City of the Legions, among the first Christian martyrs of Britain.",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the baths, the amphitheatre and the barracks. The National Roman Legion Museum next door, part of Amgueddfa Cymru, is free and holds the finds from the fortress."],
  ["## Isca, caer yr Ail Leng",
   "Caerllion ar Wysg oedd Isca, un o ddim ond tair caer barhaol i leng ym Mhrydain Rufeinig.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerleon]] Hi oedd cartref yr Ail Leng Awgwstaidd am ryw ddau gan mlynedd. Oddi yma daliai’r lleng y Silwriaid, a ymladdodd yn erbyn Rhufain yn hwy na bron neb ym Mhrydain.",
   "Yma gallwch weld o hyd faddonau’r gaer, y daliai eu pwll nofio awyr agored fwy nag 80,000 galwyn o ddŵr; yr amffitheatr, yr un fwyaf cyflawn ym Mhrydain, gyda lle i tua 6,000; a barics y milwyr, yr unig farics lleng sydd i’w gweld yn unman yn Ewrop.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerleon]]",
   "## Dinas Arthur",
   "Ni anghofiwyd Caerllion erioed. Gwelodd Gerallt Gymro ei muriau mawr, ei baddonau a’i phlastai’n dal i sefyll yn 1188, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru cynhaliai Arthur ei lys yma: mae tair rhamant Gymraeg Owain, Peredur a Geraint i gyd yn dechrau gydag Arthur yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg. Mae Gildas yn sôn am Iwl ac Aaron, a ferthyrwyd yn Ninas y Llengoedd, ymysg merthyron Cristnogol cyntaf Prydain.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y baddonau, yr amffitheatr a’r barics. Mae Amgueddfa Lleng Rufeinig Cymru drws nesaf, rhan o Amgueddfa Cymru, am ddim ac yn cadw’r darganfyddiadau o’r gaer."]),

 "caerwent": (
  ["## The town of the Silures",
   "Venta Silurum, the market town of the Silures, was laid out around AD 75 to 80 on a neat grid of streets, with a forum and basilica, public baths and a Romano-British temple.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerwent]] The Silures had held out against Rome for a generation; here their leaders became the council of their own self-governing town.",
   "Its walls still stand up to five metres high, among the finest town walls left anywhere in Roman Britain.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerwent]] The people seem to have come down from their old stronghold at Llanmelin Wood, on the hill above, which was left at about the time the town was built.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the walls, the forum, the temple and the houses, which lie among the homes of the village today. Entry is free."],
  ["## Tref y Silwriaid",
   "Gosodwyd Venta Silurum, tref farchnad y Silwriaid, tua OC 75 i 80 ar batrwm grid taclus o strydoedd, gyda fforwm a basilica, baddonau cyhoeddus a theml Rufeinig-Brydeinig.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerwent]] Roedd y Silwriaid wedi dal eu tir yn erbyn Rhufain am genhedlaeth; yma daeth eu harweinwyr yn gyngor ar eu tref hunanlywodraethol eu hunain.",
   "Mae ei muriau’n dal i sefyll hyd at bum metr o uchder, ymysg y muriau tref gorau sydd ar ôl yn unman ym Mhrydain Rufeinig.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerwent]] Ymddengys i’r bobl ddod i lawr o’u hen gadarnle yng Nghoed Llanmelin ar y bryn uwchben, a adawyd tua’r adeg y codwyd y dref.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y muriau, y fforwm, y deml a’r tai, sydd ymysg cartrefi’r pentref heddiw. Mae mynediad am ddim."]),

 "segontium": (
  ["## The fort at the mouth of the Seiont",
   "Agricola founded Segontium in AD 77, after a rising of the Ordovices, for a regiment of about a thousand auxiliary soldiers. Roman roads joined it to Chester and Caerleon, and it guarded the way to Ynys Môn.[[cite:pl-cadw-segontium]]",
   "Coins show that the Romans stayed until about AD 394, longer than at any other fort in Wales, and in its last years it helped guard the coast against raiders from Ireland.[[cite:pl-cadw-segontium]]",
   "## Macsen’s dream",
   "In the Dream of Macsen Wledig, the emperor dreams of a maiden in a fort at the mouth of the river Seiont, and crosses the world to find her.[[cite:pl-cadw-segontium]] She is Elen, and Welsh tradition remembers her as Elen Luyddog, Elen of the Hosts, who made the roads of Wales.",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the fort, on the hill above Caernarfon. Entry is free."],
  ["## Y gaer wrth aber Seiont",
   "Sefydlodd Agricola Segontium yn OC 77, ar ôl gwrthryfel yr Ordoficiaid, ar gyfer catrawd o tua mil o filwyr cynorthwyol. Cysylltai ffyrdd Rhufeinig hi â Chaer a Chaerllion, a gwarchodai’r ffordd i Ynys Môn.[[cite:pl-cadw-segontium]]",
   "Mae darnau arian yn dangos i’r Rhufeiniaid aros tan tua OC 394, yn hwy nag yn unrhyw gaer arall yng Nghymru, ac yn ei blynyddoedd olaf helpodd i warchod yr arfordir rhag ysbeilwyr o Iwerddon.[[cite:pl-cadw-segontium]]",
   "## Breuddwyd Macsen",
   "Ym Mreuddwyd Macsen Wledig, mae’r ymerawdwr yn breuddwydio am forwyn mewn caer wrth aber afon Seiont, ac yn croesi’r byd i’w chael.[[cite:pl-cadw-segontium]] Elen yw hi, a chofia traddodiad Cymru amdani fel Elen Luyddog, a wnaeth ffyrdd Cymru.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y gaer, ar y bryn uwchben Caernarfon. Mae mynediad am ddim."]),

 "dolaucothi": (
  ["## Britain’s only known Roman gold mine",
   "At Dolaucothi, by Pumsaint in the hills of Carmarthenshire, gold was mined at least two thousand years ago. It is the only Roman gold mine known in Britain.[[cite:pl-nt-dolaucothi]] A Roman fort stood at Pumsaint below; it was dug in the 1980s and 1990s, and its finds are shown on the site.[[cite:pl-nt-dolaucothi]]",
   "Mining came back in Victorian times and went on until just before the Second World War, in the 1930s.[[cite:pl-nt-dolaucothi]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "The National Trust cares for the mines. You can walk into a floodlit Roman tunnel, take a guided tour underground, pan for gold and walk the estate. The mine closes in winter, while the woodland walks stay open."],
  ["## Yr unig gloddfa aur Rufeinig hysbys ym Mhrydain",
   "Yn Nolaucothi, ger Pumsaint ym mryniau Sir Gaerfyrddin, cloddiwyd am aur o leiaf ddwy fil o flynyddoedd yn ôl. Dyma’r unig gloddfa aur Rufeinig y gwyddys amdani ym Mhrydain.[[cite:pl-nt-dolaucothi]] Safai caer Rufeinig ym Mhumsaint islaw; cloddiwyd hi yn yr 1980au a’r 1990au, a dangosir ei darganfyddiadau ar y safle.[[cite:pl-nt-dolaucothi]]",
   "Daeth y cloddio’n ôl yn oes Victoria a pharhau tan ychydig cyn yr Ail Ryfel Byd, yn y 1930au.[[cite:pl-nt-dolaucothi]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Yr Ymddiriedolaeth Genedlaethol sy’n gofalu am y cloddfeydd. Gallwch gerdded i mewn i dwnnel Rhufeinig dan olau, mynd ar daith dywys dan ddaear, chwilio am aur a cherdded yr ystad. Mae’r gloddfa ar gau yn y gaeaf, ond mae’r llwybrau coed ar agor."]),

 "ygaer": (
  ["## A fort at the crossroads",
   "Y Gaer was built around AD 75 where two great Roman roads met in the Usk valley, and it was one of the largest Roman forts inland in Wales.[[cite:pl-cadw-ygaer]] Its garrison was a cavalry regiment raised among the Vettones of Spain, up to 500 horsemen.[[cite:pl-wiki-ygaer]] The Second Legion from Caerleon rebuilt it in stone in the 100s.[[cite:pl-wiki-ygaer]]",
   "Its walls still stand nearly three metres high in places, with gateways, corner towers, a guardhouse, a granary and a heated bathhouse.[[cite:pl-cadw-ygaer]] Mortimer Wheeler dug here in the 1920s, and some of its stone may have gone into Brecon Castle after 1093.[[cite:pl-wiki-ygaer]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the fort, which lies on a working farm west of Brecon. There is no car park; reach it on foot along the lane."],
  ["## Caer ar y groesffordd",
   "Codwyd Y Gaer tua OC 75 lle cyfarfyddai dwy ffordd Rufeinig fawr yn nyffryn Wysg, ac roedd yn un o gaerau Rhufeinig mwyaf mewndirol Cymru.[[cite:pl-cadw-ygaer]] Ei gwarchodlu oedd catrawd o farchogion a godwyd ymysg y Vettones yn Sbaen, hyd at 500 o farchogion.[[cite:pl-wiki-ygaer]] Ailgododd yr Ail Leng o Gaerllion hi mewn carreg yn y 100au.[[cite:pl-wiki-ygaer]]",
   "Mae ei muriau’n dal bron yn dri metr o uchder mewn mannau, gyda phyrth, tyrau cornel, gwarchodfa, ysgubor a baddondy wedi’i gynhesu.[[cite:pl-cadw-ygaer]] Bu Mortimer Wheeler yn cloddio yma yn yr 1920au, ac efallai i rai o’i cherrig fynd i Gastell Aberhonddu ar ôl 1093.[[cite:pl-wiki-ygaer]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y gaer, sydd ar fferm weithiol i’r gorllewin o Aberhonddu. Nid oes maes parcio; cerddwch ati ar hyd y lôn."]),

 "tomenymur": (
  ["## A fort on the moors",
   "Tomen y Mur was built in AD 78 during Agricola’s campaign in the north. It was first of earth and timber, then rebuilt in stone for about 500 infantry, and left around AD 140.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur]] Here Sarn Helen met the road from Segontium to Caer Gai.[[cite:pl-cf-tomenymur]]",
   "Around it you can trace a small military amphitheatre, rare in Britain, and a levelled parade ground.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur,pl-cf-tomenymur]] The mound that gives the place its name, the Tomen, is a Norman motte raised on the fort’s western rampart, perhaps by William Rufus in 1095.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur]]",
   "## Mur Castell",
   "In the Fourth Branch of the Mabinogi this is Mur Castell, the court of Ardudwy, where Lleu Llaw Gyffes lived with Blodeuwedd, the woman made of flowers.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "A one-mile waymarked walk with information panels starts from a small car park on a lane off the A487.[[cite:pl-cf-tomenymur]] The land is open, with sheep grazing, and the views reach across to the mountains of Eryri."],
  ["## Caer ar y rhos",
   "Codwyd Tomen y Mur yn OC 78 yn ystod ymgyrch Agricola yn y gogledd. Roedd o bridd a phren i ddechrau, yna ailgodwyd hi mewn carreg ar gyfer tua 500 o filwyr traed, a’i gadael tua OC 140.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur]] Yma cyfarfyddai Sarn Helen â’r ffordd o Segontium i Gaer Gai.[[cite:pl-cf-tomenymur]]",
   "O’i chwmpas gallwch olrhain amffitheatr filwrol fach, sy’n brin ym Mhrydain, a maes parêd wedi’i lefelu.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur,pl-cf-tomenymur]] Twmpath Normanaidd ar ragfur gorllewinol y gaer yw’r domen sy’n rhoi ei henw i’r lle, efallai gan Wiliam Rufus yn 1095.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur]]",
   "## Mur Castell",
   "Ym Mhedwaredd Gainc y Mabinogi dyma Fur Castell, llys Ardudwy, lle bu Lleu Llaw Gyffes yn byw gyda Blodeuwedd, y wraig a wnaed o flodau.[[cite:pl-wiki-tomenymur]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Mae taith gerdded filltir ag arwyddion a phaneli gwybodaeth yn dechrau o faes parcio bach ar lôn oddi ar yr A487.[[cite:pl-cf-tomenymur]] Mae’r tir yn agored, gyda defaid yn pori, ac mae’r golygfeydd yn cyrraedd mynyddoedd Eryri."]),

 "moridunum": (
  ["## The town of the Demetae",
   "Moridunum was the chief town of the Demetae, the people of the south-west. A fort was built here about AD 75, perhaps in place of a hillfort on Merlin’s Hill, and around 120 the town took over, with a grid of streets, public baths, and later a bank, a ditch and a stone wall.[[cite:pl-wiki-moridunum]]",
   "Its amphitheatre, east of the old town, was dug in 1968. It is one of only seven Roman amphitheatres left in Britain, and far larger than a town of its size would seem to need.[[cite:pl-wiki-moridunum]]",
   "## Caerfyrddin",
   "The Welsh name Caerfyrddin adds caer, fort, to the old Roman name.[[cite:pl-wiki-moridunum]] Welsh tradition holds that this is Myrddin’s fort, and that Myrddin was born here.",
   "## Visiting",
   "The amphitheatre is a grassy hollow beside Priory Street, open at any time. Carmarthenshire Museum at Abergwili holds finds from the Roman town."],
  ["## Tref y Demetae",
   "Moridunum oedd prif dref y Demetae, pobl y de-orllewin. Codwyd caer yma tua OC 75, efallai yn lle bryngaer ar Fryn Myrddin, a thua 120 cymerodd y dref ei lle, gyda grid o strydoedd, baddonau cyhoeddus, ac yn ddiweddarach clawdd, ffos a mur cerrig.[[cite:pl-wiki-moridunum]]",
   "Cloddiwyd ei hamffitheatr, i’r dwyrain o’r hen dref, yn 1968. Mae’n un o ddim ond saith amffitheatr Rufeinig sydd ar ôl ym Mhrydain, ac yn llawer mwy nag y byddai tref o’i maint fel petai ei angen.[[cite:pl-wiki-moridunum]]",
   "## Caerfyrddin",
   "Mae’r enw Cymraeg Caerfyrddin yn ychwanegu caer at yr hen enw Rhufeinig.[[cite:pl-wiki-moridunum]] Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, caer Myrddin yw hon, ac yma y ganed Myrddin.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Pant glaswelltog ger Heol y Prior yw’r amffitheatr, ar agor unrhyw adeg. Mae Amgueddfa Sir Gaerfyrddin yn Abergwili yn cadw darganfyddiadau o’r dref Rufeinig."]),

 "dinlligwy": (
  ["## A village of the late Roman years",
   "Din Lligwy covers more than a quarter of a football pitch, within a stone wall: two round houses and several rectangular buildings, probably barns and workshops. Coins, pottery and glass date it to the 200s and 300s, and traces outside the wall suggest people lived here from the Iron Age.[[cite:pl-cadw-dinlligwy]]",
   "It was a farming community, and the wall probably kept in livestock rather than keeping out enemies.[[cite:pl-cadw-dinlligwy]] Here the Britons of Môn went on living their own lives while Rome ruled at Segontium across the strait.",
   "## Three ages in one walk",
   "A few minutes away stand the roofless medieval chapel of Capel Lligwy and the Lligwy burial chamber, with its enormous capstone.[[cite:pl-cadw-dinlligwy]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for all three; entry is free. They lie off the A5025, about a mile north of Llanallgo."],
  ["## Pentref o flynyddoedd olaf y Rhufeiniaid",
   "Mae Din Lligwy’n gorchuddio mwy na chwarter cae pêl-droed, o fewn mur cerrig: dau dŷ crwn a sawl adeilad petryal, ysguboriau a gweithdai yn ôl pob tebyg. Mae darnau arian, crochenwaith a gwydr yn ei ddyddio i’r 200au a’r 300au, ac mae olion y tu allan i’r mur yn awgrymu bod pobl yn byw yma o Oes yr Haearn.[[cite:pl-cadw-dinlligwy]]",
   "Cymuned ffermio oedd hi, a chadw da byw i mewn yn hytrach na chadw gelynion allan oedd gwaith y mur yn ôl pob tebyg.[[cite:pl-cadw-dinlligwy]] Yma aeth Brythoniaid Môn ymlaen â’u bywydau eu hunain tra rheolai Rhufain yn Segontium dros y Fenai.",
   "## Tair oes mewn un daith",
   "Ychydig funudau i ffwrdd saif Capel Lligwy, capel canoloesol heb do, a siambr gladdu Lligwy, â’i maen capan anferth.[[cite:pl-cadw-dinlligwy]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y tri; mae mynediad am ddim. Maen nhw oddi ar yr A5025, tua milltir i’r gogledd o Lanallgo."]),

 # ---------- hillforts ----------
 "trerceiri": (
  ["## The town of the giants",
   "Tre’r Ceiri, the town of the giants, stands 450 metres up on Yr Eifl above the Llŷn coast. It was first built around 200 BC, but most finds date from AD 150 to 400: the Britons of Llŷn went on living here all through the Roman years.[[cite:pl-wiki-trerceiri]]",
   "Inside walls that still stand up to four metres high are the ruins of about 150 stone houses, which once had roofs of turf. In Roman times it may have been home to as many as 400 people.[[cite:pl-wiki-trerceiri]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "A steep path climbs from the road between Llanaelhaearn and Llithfaen. Keep to the paths and don’t move any stones: the walls are fragile."],
  ["## Tref y cewri",
   "Saif Tre’r Ceiri 450 metr i fyny ar Yr Eifl uwchben arfordir Llŷn. Fe’i codwyd gyntaf tua 200 CC, ond mae’r rhan fwyaf o’r darganfyddiadau’n dyddio o OC 150 i 400: aeth Brythoniaid Llŷn ymlaen i fyw yma drwy holl flynyddoedd y Rhufeiniaid.[[cite:pl-wiki-trerceiri]]",
   "O fewn muriau sy’n dal hyd at bedwar metr o uchder mae adfeilion tua 150 o dai cerrig, a fu unwaith â thoeau tywyrch. Yn oes y Rhufeiniaid efallai i gynifer â 400 o bobl fyw yma.[[cite:pl-wiki-trerceiri]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Mae llwybr serth yn dringo o’r ffordd rhwng Llanaelhaearn a Llithfaen. Cadwch at y llwybrau a pheidiwch â symud cerrig: mae’r muriau’n fregus."]),

 "castellhenllys": (
  ["## A village of the Demetae",
   "Castell Henllys is one of the most thoroughly dug and studied Iron Age sites in Britain. Archaeologists and volunteers have rebuilt its roundhouses on the very spot where they stood two thousand years ago, using the same kinds of materials.[[cite:pl-pcnp-henllys]]",
   "The people who lived here were the Demetae, the Britons of the south-west, before, during and after the Roman conquest.[[cite:pl-pcnp-henllys]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "The Pembrokeshire Coast National Park Authority owns and runs the village. There is an entry charge, and costumed guides bring the Iron Age to life."],
  ["## Pentref y Demetae",
   "Mae Castell Henllys yn un o’r safleoedd Oes yr Haearn a gloddiwyd ac a astudiwyd fwyaf trylwyr ym Mhrydain. Mae archaeolegwyr a gwirfoddolwyr wedi ailgodi ei thai crwn ar yr union fan lle safent ddwy fil o flynyddoedd yn ôl, gan ddefnyddio’r un math o ddeunyddiau.[[cite:pl-pcnp-henllys]]",
   "Y Demetae, Brythoniaid y de-orllewin, oedd yn byw yma, cyn, yn ystod ac ar ôl y goresgyniad Rhufeinig.[[cite:pl-pcnp-henllys]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Awdurdod Parc Cenedlaethol Arfordir Penfro sy’n berchen ar y pentref ac yn ei redeg. Mae tâl mynediad, ac mae tywyswyr mewn gwisg yn dod ag Oes yr Haearn yn fyw."]),

 "llanmelin": (
  ["## The stronghold of the Silures",
   "Llanmelin was built by Britons in the Iron Age, before the Romans came. Digging found that its people lived in round houses of timber and mud, kept livestock, made pottery, smelted copper and carved red deer antler.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]",
   "The fort seems to have been left around AD 75, just when the Roman town of Caerwent was founded below. Its people may have moved down to become the townsfolk of Venta Silurum, the market town of the Silures.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the earthworks in the wood: a main camp, an annexe and an outpost about 250 metres away. Entry is free, in daylight hours.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]"],
  ["## Cadarnle’r Silwriaid",
   "Codwyd Llanmelin gan Frythoniaid yn Oes yr Haearn, cyn i’r Rhufeiniaid ddod. Dangosodd cloddio fod ei phobl yn byw mewn tai crwn o bren a llaid, yn cadw da byw, yn gwneud crochenwaith, yn mwyndoddi copr ac yn cerfio cyrn carw coch.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]",
   "Ymddengys i’r gaer gael ei gadael tua OC 75, yr union adeg y sefydlwyd tref Rufeinig Caer-went islaw. Efallai i’w phobl symud i lawr i fod yn drigolion Venta Silurum, tref farchnad y Silwriaid.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y cloddiau yn y coed: prif wersyll, atodiad a gwylfa tua 250 metr i ffwrdd. Mae mynediad am ddim, yng ngolau dydd.[[cite:pl-cadw-llanmelin]]"]),

 "caerytwr": (
  ["## A fort on the edge of Wales",
   "Caer y Twr encloses some seventeen acres on the summit of Holyhead Mountain, with ramparts that still stand three metres high and up to four metres thick.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerytwr]]",
   "The only building inside is a later Roman watchtower and signal station, which may have worked with the fort of Caer Gybi below to watch the Irish Sea.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerytwr]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for it; it is free and open in daylight. Paths climb from the South Stack road, and the round houses of Tŷ Mawr are on the slopes below.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]"],
  ["## Caer ar ymyl Cymru",
   "Mae Caer y Twr yn amgáu rhyw ddwy erw ar bymtheg ar gopa Mynydd Twr, gyda rhagfuriau sy’n dal yn dri metr o uchder a hyd at bedwar metr o drwch.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerytwr]]",
   "Yr unig adeilad y tu mewn yw gwylfa a gorsaf signal Rufeinig ddiweddarach, a allai fod wedi cydweithio â chaer Caergybi islaw i wylio Môr Iwerddon.[[cite:pl-cadw-caerytwr]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu amdani; mae am ddim ac ar agor yng ngolau dydd. Mae llwybrau’n dringo o ffordd Ynys Lawd, ac mae tai crwn Tŷ Mawr ar y llethrau islaw.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]"]),

 "tymawr": (
  ["## A village by South Stack",
   "On the slopes of Holyhead Mountain about twenty round houses survive of a village that once had perhaps fifty. They were built of stone with tall thatched roofs, and some still show divisions inside, storage places, stone benches and basins.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]",
   "Roman coins and pottery were found here, and the village was once thought to be Roman; it is now known to be older, a farming village used by many generations.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the huts; entry is free. They are beside the road to South Stack, and Caer y Twr is on the summit above.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]"],
  ["## Pentref ger Ynys Lawd",
   "Ar lethrau Mynydd Twr mae tua ugain o dai crwn wedi goroesi o bentref a fu unwaith â hanner cant efallai. Fe’u codwyd o gerrig gyda thoeau gwellt uchel, ac mae rhai’n dal i ddangos rhaniadau y tu mewn, mannau storio, meinciau a basnau cerrig.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]",
   "Cafwyd darnau arian a chrochenwaith Rhufeinig yma, a chredid unwaith mai Rhufeinig oedd y pentref; gwyddys yn awr ei fod yn hŷn, yn bentref ffermio a ddefnyddiwyd gan genedlaethau lawer.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y cytiau; mae mynediad am ddim. Maen nhw ger y ffordd i Ynys Lawd, ac mae Caer y Twr ar y copa uwchben.[[cite:pl-cadw-tymawr]]"]),

 "caerdrewyn": (
  ["## A fort of the Iron Age",
   "Caer Drewyn is an Iron Age hillfort of about 500 BC on a spur above Corwen, between Llangollen and Corwen. It covers about three hectares, and its later dry-stone wall may once have stood some four and a half metres high.[[cite:pl-wiki-caerdrewyn]]",
   "## Two Owains",
   "It stayed a stronghold long after the Iron Age.[[cite:pl-wiki-caerdrewyn]] Tradition holds that Owain Gwynedd held the fort in 1165, when Henry II came against Wales and was turned back on the Berwyn, and that in 1400, after his followers proclaimed him Prince of Wales at Glyndyfrdwy, Owain Glyndŵr gathered his men here.[[cite:pl-wiki-caerdrewyn]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "The fort is on farmland above the Dee, north of Corwen. Keep to the public paths."],
  ["## Caer o Oes yr Haearn",
   "Bryngaer o Oes yr Haearn tua 500 CC yw Caer Drewyn, ar ysgwydd o fryn uwchben Corwen, rhwng Llangollen a Chorwen. Mae’n gorchuddio tua thri hectar, ac efallai i’w mur sych diweddarach sefyll unwaith ryw bedwar metr a hanner o uchder.[[cite:pl-wiki-caerdrewyn]]",
   "## Dau Owain",
   "Arhosodd yn gadarnle ymhell ar ôl Oes yr Haearn.[[cite:pl-wiki-caerdrewyn]] Yn ôl traddodiad daliai Owain Gwynedd y gaer yn 1165, pan ddaeth Harri II yn erbyn Cymru a chael ei droi’n ôl ar y Berwyn, ac yn 1400, ar ôl i’w ddilynwyr ei gyhoeddi’n Dywysog Cymru yng Nglyndyfrdwy, casglodd Owain Glyndŵr ei wŷr yma.[[cite:pl-wiki-caerdrewyn]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Mae’r gaer ar dir fferm uwchben afon Dyfrdwy, i’r gogledd o Gorwen. Cadwch at y llwybrau cyhoeddus."]),

 # ---------- tombs and treasures ----------
 "brynceliddu": (
  ["## The tomb of the longest day",
   "Bryn Celli Ddu began as a henge, a bank and ditch around a circle of stones, in the Neolithic. Later a chambered tomb was built in its place, under a mound up to 26 metres across.[[cite:pl-cadw-brynceliddu]]",
   "A long, narrow passage leads to an eight-sided chamber, where human bones, arrowheads and carved stones have been found. At sunrise on midsummer’s day, shafts of light shine straight down the passage into the chamber.[[cite:pl-cadw-brynceliddu]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the tomb, a short walk from the lane near Llanddaniel Fab. Entry is free."],
  ["## Siambr y dydd hwyaf",
   "Dechreuodd Bryn Celli Ddu fel hengor, clawdd a ffos o amgylch cylch o gerrig, yn yr oes Neolithig. Yn ddiweddarach codwyd siambr gladdu yn ei le, dan dwmpath hyd at 26 metr ar draws.[[cite:pl-cadw-brynceliddu]]",
   "Mae cyntedd hir, cul yn arwain at siambr wyth ochr, lle cafwyd esgyrn dynol, pennau saethau a cherrig cerfiedig. Ar godiad haul ar ddydd hirddydd haf, mae pelydrau o oleuni’n disgleirio’n syth i lawr y cyntedd i’r siambr.[[cite:pl-cadw-brynceliddu]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y siambr, ychydig gamau o’r lôn ger Llanddaniel Fab. Mae mynediad am ddim."]),

 "pentreifan": (
  ["## Five thousand years on three stones",
   "Pentre Ifan has stood for more than five thousand years. Its capstone, about five metres long, rests on three upright stones; once the whole frame was covered by a mound of earth.[[cite:pl-cadw-pentreifan]]",
   "It looks out over the Preseli Hills, the mountains whose bluestones were carried to Stonehenge.",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for it; entry is free. It is near Nevern, a short walk from the lane."],
  ["## Pum mil o flynyddoedd ar dri maen",
   "Mae Pentre Ifan wedi sefyll am fwy na phum mil o flynyddoedd. Mae ei faen capan, tua phum metr o hyd, yn gorffwys ar dri maen unionsyth; unwaith roedd y fframwaith cyfan dan dwmpath o bridd.[[cite:pl-cadw-pentreifan]]",
   "Mae’n edrych dros y Preseli, y mynyddoedd y cludwyd eu cerrig gleision i Gôr y Cewri.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu amdano; mae mynediad am ddim. Mae ger Nanhyfer, ychydig gamau o’r lôn."]),

 "barclodiad": (
  ["## The Giantess’s Apronful",
   "Under a great mound on the headland above Porth Trecastell, a passage seven metres long leads to a cross-shaped chamber. Five of its stones are carved with zig-zags and spirals.[[cite:pl-cadw-barclodiad]]",
   "Carvings like these are found at the great tombs of Ireland, and the only other tomb in Britain with such art is Bryn Celli Ddu, a few miles away.[[cite:pl-cadw-barclodiad]] The farmers of Môn and Ireland shared one sea and one way of honouring their dead.",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the tomb; it is free, and you can see it from outside all year. Cadw runs guided tours inside on set summer days. Park at Porth Trecastell and take the coast path, with care where the cliff is eroding.[[cite:pl-cadw-barclodiad]]"],
  ["## Barclodiad y Gawres",
   "Dan dwmpath mawr ar y pentir uwchben Porth Trecastell, mae cyntedd saith metr o hyd yn arwain at siambr ar ffurf croes. Mae pump o’i cherrig wedi’u cerfio â igam-ogamau a throellau.[[cite:pl-cadw-barclodiad]]",
   "Ceir cerfiadau fel hyn yn siambrau mawr Iwerddon, a’r unig siambr arall ym Mhrydain â chelf o’r fath yw Bryn Celli Ddu, ychydig filltiroedd i ffwrdd.[[cite:pl-cadw-barclodiad]] Rhannai ffermwyr Môn ac Iwerddon un môr ac un ffordd o anrhydeddu eu meirw.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y siambr; mae am ddim, a gallwch ei gweld o’r tu allan drwy’r flwyddyn. Mae Cadw’n cynnal teithiau tywys y tu mewn ar ddyddiau penodol yn yr haf. Parciwch ym Mhorth Trecastell a dilyn llwybr yr arfordir, yn ofalus lle mae’r clogwyn yn erydu.[[cite:pl-cadw-barclodiad]]"]),

 "tinkinswood": (
  ["## A capstone of forty tons",
   "The capstone of Tinkinswood is about seven metres long and weighs some forty tons, as much as a loaded lorry; at least 200 people would have been needed to raise it.[[cite:pl-cadw-tinkinswood]] Digging found the remains of more than fifty people, with broken pottery and flint tools.[[cite:pl-cadw-tinkinswood]]",
   "## A night at the stones",
   "The old story says that whoever spends the night here on May Eve, St John’s Eve or Midwinter Eve will die, go mad, or become a poet.[[cite:pl-cadw-tinkinswood]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the tomb, near St Nicholas in the Vale of Glamorgan; entry is free. St Lythans burial chamber is a short drive away."],
  ["## Maen capan deugain tunnell",
   "Mae maen capan Tinkinswood tua saith metr o hyd ac yn pwyso rhyw ddeugain tunnell, cymaint â lori lawn; byddai angen o leiaf 200 o bobl i’w godi.[[cite:pl-cadw-tinkinswood]] Cafwyd gweddillion mwy na hanner cant o bobl, gyda chrochenwaith toredig ac offer fflint.[[cite:pl-cadw-tinkinswood]]",
   "## Noson wrth y meini",
   "Mae’r hen stori’n dweud y bydd pwy bynnag a dreulia’r nos yma ar Nos Galan Mai, Noswyl Ifan neu Nos Ganol Gaeaf yn marw, yn mynd yn wallgof, neu’n dod yn fardd.[[cite:pl-cadw-tinkinswood]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y siambr, ger Sain Nicolas ym Mro Morgannwg; mae mynediad am ddim. Mae siambr gladdu Llwyneliddon daith fer i ffwrdd."]),

 "maenceti": (
  ["## Arthur’s Stone",
   "Maen Ceti is a Neolithic burial chamber just north of the summit of Cefn Bryn, the long ridge at the heart of Gower.[[cite:pl-wiki-maenceti]] Its English name, Arthur’s Stone, comes from the old story that Arthur threw the great stone here from Llanelli, across the estuary.[[cite:pl-wiki-maenceti]]",
   "It is a scheduled monument, protected by Cadw.[[cite:pl-aubrey-maenceti]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "The stone is on open common land, a short walk north from the Reynoldston road across Cefn Bryn, with views over the Loughor estuary."],
  ["## Maen Ceti",
   "Siambr gladdu Neolithig ychydig i’r gogledd o gopa Cefn Bryn, y gefnen hir yng nghanol Gŵyr, yw Maen Ceti.[[cite:pl-wiki-maenceti]] Daw ei enw Saesneg, Arthur’s Stone, o’r hen stori mai Arthur a daflodd y maen mawr yma o Lanelli, ar draws yr aber.[[cite:pl-wiki-maenceti]]",
   "Mae’n heneb gofrestredig, wedi’i gwarchod gan Cadw.[[cite:pl-aubrey-maenceti]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Mae’r maen ar dir comin agored, taith gerdded fer i’r gogledd o ffordd Reynoldston ar draws Cefn Bryn, gyda golygfeydd dros aber Llwchwr."]),

 "parclebreos": (
  ["## A tomb found by roadmen",
   "In 1869 workmen digging for road stone in a quiet Gower valley broke into a long cairn. Inside, later digging found the bones of at least forty people.[[cite:pl-cadw-parclebreos]] It is one of the finest tombs of the first farmers in Wales.",
   "## Visiting",
   "Cadw cares for the tomb; entry is free. It is about a mile and a half north-north-east of Penmaen, off the A4118, on foot through Parc le Breos.[[cite:pl-cadw-parclebreos]]"],
  ["## Siambr a gafwyd gan weithwyr ffordd",
   "Yn 1869 torrodd gweithwyr a oedd yn cloddio am gerrig ffordd mewn cwm tawel yng Ngŵyr i mewn i garnedd hir. Y tu mewn, cafodd cloddio diweddarach esgyrn o leiaf deugain o bobl.[[cite:pl-cadw-parclebreos]] Mae’n un o siambrau gorau’r ffermwyr cyntaf yng Nghymru.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cadw sy’n gofalu am y siambr; mae mynediad am ddim. Mae tua milltir a hanner i’r gogledd-gogledd-ddwyrain o Benmaen, oddi ar yr A4118, ar droed drwy Barc le Breos.[[cite:pl-cadw-parclebreos]]"]),

 "carregsamson": (
  ["## Samson’s stone",
   "Carreg Samson stands about half a mile west of Abercastle, near the Pembrokeshire Coast Path. It is some five thousand years old. Its capstone, almost five metres long and a metre thick, rests on three of six upright stones.[[cite:pl-wiki-carregsamson]]",
   "Tradition holds that Saint Samson of Dol, the pupil of Illtud, set the capstone in place with his little finger.[[cite:pl-wiki-carregsamson]] Digging in 1968 found the holes of more stones, a pit, burnt bone, pottery and flints.[[cite:pl-wiki-carregsamson]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "The dolmen stands in a field near the Coast Path west of Abercastle, with a view over the sea."],
  ["## Carreg Samson",
   "Saif Carreg Samson tua hanner milltir i’r gorllewin o Abercastell, ger Llwybr Arfordir Penfro. Mae tua phum mil o flynyddoedd oed. Mae ei maen capan, bron yn bum metr o hyd ac yn fetr o drwch, yn gorffwys ar dri o chwe maen unionsyth.[[cite:pl-wiki-carregsamson]]",
   "Yn ôl traddodiad gosododd Samson Sant o Dol, disgybl Illtud, y maen capan yn ei le â’i fys bach.[[cite:pl-wiki-carregsamson]] Cafodd cloddio yn 1968 dyllau rhagor o gerrig, pydew, asgwrn wedi’i losgi, crochenwaith a fflint.[[cite:pl-wiki-carregsamson]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Saif y gromlech mewn cae ger Llwybr yr Arfordir i’r gorllewin o Abercastell, gyda golygfa dros y môr."]),

 "mold": (
  ["## The gold cape",
   "In 1833 workmen at Bryn yr Ellyllon, a Bronze Age burial mound near Mold, found a cape of solid sheet gold, beaten from a single ingot and decorated with rows of ribs and bosses. It was made around 1900 to 1600 BC.[[cite:pl-wiki-moldcape]]",
   "Once some 200 to 300 amber beads hung on it in rows; only one survives. Nothing like it has been found anywhere else.[[cite:pl-wiki-moldcape]]",
   "## Where it is now",
   "The British Museum has held most of the cape since 1836. It has come home on loan, to Cardiff and Wrexham in 2013, and small pieces are kept at the Daniel Owen Centre in Mold. Welsh politicians have often called for it to come back to Wales for good.[[cite:pl-wiki-moldcape]]"],
  ["## Y fantell aur",
   "Yn 1833 cafodd gweithwyr ym Mryn yr Ellyllon, twmpath claddu o’r Oes Efydd ger yr Wyddgrug, fantell o ddalen aur solet, wedi’i churo o un ingot a’i haddurno â rhesi o asennau a chnapiau. Fe’i gwnaed tua 1900 i 1600 CC.[[cite:pl-wiki-moldcape]]",
   "Unwaith roedd rhyw 200 i 300 o fwclis ambr yn hongian arni mewn rhesi; dim ond un sydd wedi goroesi. Ni chafwyd dim tebyg iddi yn unman arall.[[cite:pl-wiki-moldcape]]",
   "## Ble mae hi heddiw",
   "Mae’r Amgueddfa Brydeinig wedi cadw’r rhan fwyaf o’r fantell ers 1836. Mae wedi dod adref ar fenthyg, i Gaerdydd a Wrecsam yn 2013, a chedwir darnau bach yng Nghanolfan Daniel Owen yn yr Wyddgrug. Mae gwleidyddion Cymru wedi galw droeon iddi ddod yn ôl i Gymru am byth.[[cite:pl-wiki-moldcape]]"]),

 "cerrigbach": (
  ["## Treasure in the peat",
   "In 1942, while peat was being cleared to lengthen a runway at RAF Valley, the head groundsman found the first of more than 150 Iron Age metal objects in the bed of a small lake.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]",
   "There were swords, spearheads, parts of shields, a bronze trumpet, wagon tyres, horse gear, a blacksmith’s tools, cauldrons, and an iron gang chain with five neck-rings. They are the most important collection of their kind in Wales, given to the lake over a long time, from about 300 BC to AD 100.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]",
   "## Môn of the druids",
   "Tacitus tells how the Romans crossed to Môn in AD 60 and met the druids on the shore. Cyril Fox saw the offerings of Llyn Cerrig Bach as the work of that same druid island.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]",
   "## Visiting",
   "The lake is beside the airfield at Valley. Almost all the finds are at National Museum Cardiff, part of Amgueddfa Cymru.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]"],
  ["## Trysor yn y mawn",
   "Yn 1942, wrth glirio mawn i estyn rhedfa yn RAF y Fali, cafodd y prif dirmon y cyntaf o fwy na 150 o wrthrychau metel o Oes yr Haearn yng ngwely llyn bach.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]",
   "Roedd yno gleddyfau, pennau gwaywffyn, darnau o darianau, utgorn efydd, teiars wagen, harnais ceffyl, offer gof, crochanau, a chadwyn gaethion haearn â phum cylch gwddf. Dyma’r casgliad pwysicaf o’i fath yng Nghymru, a roddwyd i’r llyn dros gyfnod hir, o tua 300 CC i OC 100.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]",
   "## Môn y derwyddon",
   "Mae Tacitus yn adrodd sut y croesodd y Rhufeiniaid i Fôn yn OC 60 a chyfarfod y derwyddon ar y lan. Gwelodd Cyril Fox offrymau Llyn Cerrig Bach yn waith yr un ynys derwyddol honno.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Mae’r llyn ger maes awyr y Fali. Mae bron y cyfan o’r darganfyddiadau yn Amgueddfa Genedlaethol Caerdydd, rhan o Amgueddfa Cymru.[[cite:pl-wiki-cerrigbach]]"]),
})

